1
00:00:00,665 --> 00:00:04,642
Hi everyone, and welcome to
Conf42 Platform Engineering.

2
00:00:04,726 --> 00:00:08,472
I am Eleftheria Batsou, Developer
Advocate at Uptime Robot.

3
00:00:08,927 --> 00:00:12,600
I work on the monitor side
of the things people ship.

4
00:00:13,130 --> 00:00:18,436
This talk is called Monitoring is
Still the One Thing Nobody Automated.

5
00:00:18,486 --> 00:00:21,903
It's a lightning talk, so
I'll keep it practical.

6
00:00:21,975 --> 00:00:28,344
We will look at why monitoring keeps
failing off the golden path, how to

7
00:00:28,344 --> 00:00:33,445
put it back as a deploy step from the
command line, the small details that bite

8
00:00:33,445 --> 00:00:38,842
you the first time, and when you should
reach for something else than the CLI.

9
00:00:39,442 --> 00:00:45,516
The examples come from Uptime Robot
because that's what I work on every day.

10
00:00:45,775 --> 00:00:51,494
But the argument is about where monitoring
sits in your platform workflow, and

11
00:00:51,526 --> 00:00:58,917
that applies whatever tool you're
using Okay, so let's get started.

12
00:01:00,036 --> 00:01:05,151
And let's start with the golden path
most platform teams have already built.

13
00:01:05,623 --> 00:01:10,347
You run one command, and you
get a repo from a template.

14
00:01:10,714 --> 00:01:12,712
CI and CD come with it.

15
00:01:13,068 --> 00:01:14,435
Secrets get injected.

16
00:01:14,483 --> 00:01:16,359
Infrastructure is code.

17
00:01:16,691 --> 00:01:20,417
Logs and dashboards are
wired up automatically.

18
00:01:20,657 --> 00:01:21,989
All of it is a default.

19
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:24,673
Nobody has to remember anything.

20
00:01:25,599 --> 00:01:32,755
Then the service goes live, and monitoring
gets set up by a human, later, by

21
00:01:32,771 --> 00:01:35,951
hand in a web UI, if anyone remembers.

22
00:01:36,481 --> 00:01:39,632
That last box is what this talk is about.

23
00:01:39,690 --> 00:01:44,782
it's the only step on this path that still
depends on memory instead of defaults.

24
00:01:46,208 --> 00:01:50,544
In production, that gap
shows up in three ways.

25
00:01:50,690 --> 00:01:52,562
First, no monitor at all.

26
00:01:52,625 --> 00:01:56,589
The service runs for months,
and the first alert you get is a

27
00:01:56,591 --> 00:01:58,466
support ticket from a customer.

28
00:01:59,342 --> 00:02:01,135
Second, the wrong monitor.

29
00:02:01,473 --> 00:02:06,314
Someone did add a check, but it hits
the marketing homepage instead of the

30
00:02:06,315 --> 00:02:13,496
service's health endpoint, or it alerts
a person who left the team last spring.

31
00:02:13,847 --> 00:02:16,839
It's green, but it isn't
telling you anything.

32
00:02:17,550 --> 00:02:19,592
Third, orphaned monitors.

33
00:02:19,762 --> 00:02:24,649
The service was renamed or retired,
and the monitor is still there.

34
00:02:24,998 --> 00:02:30,019
Either it alerts about nothing,
or it quietly checks a URL that

35
00:02:30,031 --> 00:02:31,866
now belongs to something else.

36
00:02:32,539 --> 00:02:38,098
Three different symptoms, one
root cause, and that root cause

37
00:02:38,116 --> 00:02:40,194
is not a tooling problem.

38
00:02:40,262 --> 00:02:44,349
Every monitoring tool out there
can check an HTTP endpoint.

39
00:02:45,855 --> 00:02:46,647
Excuse me.

40
00:02:47,382 --> 00:02:48,757
It's a workflow problem.

41
00:02:48,985 --> 00:02:54,418
Monitoring sits outside the golden path,
so it depends on discipline instead

42
00:02:54,418 --> 00:02:59,633
of defaults, and discipline doesn't
scale with the number of services.

43
00:02:59,976 --> 00:03:05,194
If your platform creates 50 services
a year, you need 50 separate acts

44
00:03:05,226 --> 00:03:11,071
of remembering, or you need one
default So the fix is simple to say.

45
00:03:11,536 --> 00:03:13,818
Move monitoring inside the deploy.

46
00:03:14,190 --> 00:03:19,351
Build, test, deploy, then the step
that re-registers the monitor,

47
00:03:19,392 --> 00:03:22,916
and the step that confirms the
service is actually reporting up.

48
00:03:23,493 --> 00:03:27,214
The deploy isn't done until
the service is being watched.

49
00:03:27,839 --> 00:03:30,005
That's a nice side effect.

50
00:03:30,173 --> 00:03:34,678
The monitor is now created by the
same pipeline that already knows

51
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:39,853
the service's name, its URL, its
owning team, and its environment.

52
00:03:39,943 --> 00:03:44,395
Everything a human would have to
copy into a web form is already

53
00:03:44,395 --> 00:03:46,368
sitting there as a variable.

54
00:03:47,820 --> 00:03:52,005
At Uptime Robot, we shipped an
official command line tool for this.

55
00:03:52,118 --> 00:03:53,106
It's open source.

56
00:03:53,137 --> 00:04:00,095
It's on NPM as Uptime Robot/CLI,
and it talks to our V3 API.

57
00:04:00,599 --> 00:04:03,659
You install it with NPM on your laptop.

58
00:04:03,690 --> 00:04:08,399
Uptime Robot auth login stores your key
in the operating system's key chain.

59
00:04:08,698 --> 00:04:10,198
In CI, you don't log in.

60
00:04:10,236 --> 00:04:14,796
You set up the Uptime
Robot_API_key environment

61
00:04:14,799 --> 00:04:17,149
variables from your secret store.

62
00:04:17,349 --> 00:04:21,549
Creating a monitor is then one
command, the monitor type, a name,

63
00:04:21,595 --> 00:04:24,205
a URL, an interval, and a timeout.

64
00:04:24,583 --> 00:04:26,807
Keep an eye on that timeout flag.

65
00:04:26,823 --> 00:04:33,586
We'll come back to it And, yes,
here's actually the part I like most.

66
00:04:33,672 --> 00:04:40,104
Add dry dash run to any create command,
and the CLI compiles and validates

67
00:04:40,123 --> 00:04:44,429
the request without authenticating
and without touching the network.

68
00:04:44,828 --> 00:04:50,480
On the right side, it prints the
exact post to v3/monitors and the body

69
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:55,316
it would send, the name, interval,
URL, timeout, the HTTP method,

70
00:04:55,389 --> 00:04:58,052
the tags, and the alert contacts.

71
00:04:58,400 --> 00:05:03,579
And actually, let me
show you on my screen.

72
00:05:03,579 --> 00:05:05,490
Let me change.

73
00:05:05,543 --> 00:05:06,013
Okay.

74
00:05:06,403 --> 00:05:08,939
So let's actually do a very short demo.

75
00:05:08,939 --> 00:05:14,569
Here you can already see the monitors
that I've been watching in Uptime Robot.

76
00:05:14,629 --> 00:05:19,524
I am currently on our CLI,
and here is the result.

77
00:05:20,175 --> 00:05:24,958
Because it needs no credential, you
can run this in a pull request check.

78
00:05:25,048 --> 00:05:29,188
A services monitoring config gets
validated before a merge, the same

79
00:05:29,188 --> 00:05:31,325
way you would lint a Docker file.

80
00:05:31,733 --> 00:05:36,286
If the request is wrong, for example,
if I leave it out the timeout, it

81
00:05:36,291 --> 00:05:40,398
exits with code two and tells you
exactly which fields are missing.

82
00:05:40,503 --> 00:05:42,203
So yeah, that was it.

83
00:05:42,675 --> 00:05:46,237
Let's continue back to the slides.

84
00:05:46,273 --> 00:05:48,039
Let's change slide.

85
00:05:48,059 --> 00:05:48,667
Great.

86
00:05:49,541 --> 00:05:52,225
So what goes into that command?

87
00:05:52,237 --> 00:05:55,302
This is where a platform
team should be opinionated.

88
00:05:55,707 --> 00:05:59,731
On the left is what belongs
in the template, decided

89
00:05:59,744 --> 00:06:01,595
once by the platform team.

90
00:06:01,981 --> 00:06:06,596
The check interval for each tier,
the timeout, the HTTP method.

91
00:06:06,991 --> 00:06:11,974
I would default to GET, and I'll
explain why in a minute, which response

92
00:06:11,979 --> 00:06:18,088
codes count as success, SSL and domain
expiry reminders, the tag convention,

93
00:06:18,105 --> 00:06:20,770
and how alert contacts map to teams.

94
00:06:21,315 --> 00:06:26,719
On the right side is what genuinely
varies per service, and it should

95
00:06:26,774 --> 00:06:32,056
come from the service's own metadata,
its name and environment, its health

96
00:06:32,065 --> 00:06:37,244
endpoint, its owning team, its tier,
and for scheduled job, the schedule.

97
00:06:37,634 --> 00:06:42,048
A simple rule, if you find yourself
copying the same value into every

98
00:06:42,048 --> 00:06:45,989
service's config, it belongs to the left.

99
00:06:46,112 --> 00:06:53,659
Now, deploys run more than once, so this
tab has to be safe to run again and again.

100
00:06:54,167 --> 00:06:56,630
Two facts about the CLI matter here.

101
00:06:56,691 --> 00:07:02,448
It retries reads, but it deliberately
never retries, post, patch, or delete,

102
00:07:02,775 --> 00:07:05,379
and there is no built-in upsert.

103
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:10,683
So if your pipeline calls create on
every deploy, you get a brand-new

104
00:07:10,684 --> 00:07:13,216
duplicate monitor on every deploy.

105
00:07:13,666 --> 00:07:16,153
The pattern on the left fixes that.

106
00:07:16,485 --> 00:07:18,305
Look the monitor up first.

107
00:07:18,375 --> 00:07:22,433
The list command can filter by
name, URL, tags, and status.

108
00:07:22,797 --> 00:07:24,607
Create it only if it's missing.

109
00:07:24,697 --> 00:07:26,991
Otherwise, update it in place.

110
00:07:27,498 --> 00:07:32,916
One small catch, the name filter is
the partial match, so match the exact

111
00:07:32,916 --> 00:07:40,945
name yourself like the AWK line does
here, and use a predictable unique name.

112
00:07:41,236 --> 00:07:44,235
I like service name plus environment.

113
00:07:45,034 --> 00:07:48,416
And here are the four details
that bite people the first

114
00:07:48,428 --> 00:07:51,794
time Credentials is number one.

115
00:07:52,195 --> 00:07:55,279
Creating monitors need your main API key.

116
00:07:55,319 --> 00:07:59,842
A read-only key is great for dashboards
and reports, but it can't create anything.

117
00:08:00,112 --> 00:08:04,180
You get a 403 and exit code
five, so keep two keys and give

118
00:08:04,205 --> 00:08:06,030
the pipeline the right one.

119
00:08:06,612 --> 00:08:08,156
Then required flags.

120
00:08:08,340 --> 00:08:12,588
For HTTP monitors, interval
and timeout are both required.

121
00:08:13,118 --> 00:08:15,077
Everyone forgets timeout.

122
00:08:15,128 --> 00:08:19,179
the CLI fails fast with a clear
message, which is exactly why

123
00:08:19,190 --> 00:08:21,638
the dry run belongs in CI.

124
00:08:22,420 --> 00:08:23,668
Then plan limits.

125
00:08:23,752 --> 00:08:26,793
The minimum check interval
depends on your plan.

126
00:08:27,100 --> 00:08:31,183
On the free plan, it's five minutes,
so a template that hard-codes 30

127
00:08:31,185 --> 00:08:33,687
seconds will fail for every team.

128
00:08:34,254 --> 00:08:35,264
And defaults.

129
00:08:35,282 --> 00:08:39,295
If you don't pass a method, HTTP
monitors send a HEAD request.

130
00:08:39,626 --> 00:08:42,671
Plan your framework's route
HEAD differently from GET.

131
00:08:43,098 --> 00:08:48,377
Pass Dashdash method GET expli-
ex- sorry, explicitly, and check

132
00:08:48,391 --> 00:08:50,946
what your users actually hit.

133
00:08:51,225 --> 00:08:55,569
Because this runs in a pipeline,
exit codes matter, and the

134
00:08:55,573 --> 00:08:57,999
CLI gives you distinct ones.

135
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:00,593
Exit two is invalid input.

136
00:09:00,666 --> 00:09:03,960
That's a config bug, so fail the build.

137
00:09:04,346 --> 00:09:08,326
Four and five are authentication
and permission, a wrong key

138
00:09:08,352 --> 00:09:09,823
or the wrong type of key.

139
00:09:10,214 --> 00:09:14,057
Seven is a rate limiting, which
is safe to retry with backoff,

140
00:09:14,401 --> 00:09:16,397
and eight is a conflict.

141
00:09:16,910 --> 00:09:22,561
Errors come out in standard error
JSON, so your pipeline can parse

142
00:09:22,576 --> 00:09:24,572
them instead of scraping text.

143
00:09:25,110 --> 00:09:29,698
my recommendation, block the deploy
and config errors because those mean

144
00:09:29,710 --> 00:09:31,837
the service will ship unmonitored.

145
00:09:32,271 --> 00:09:36,228
But don't block a release just
because a third-party API is

146
00:09:36,448 --> 00:09:38,098
briefly rate limiting you.

147
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:41,528
Warn, retry, and follow up.

148
00:09:41,785 --> 00:09:43,791
Not everything has a URL.

149
00:09:43,853 --> 00:09:48,387
Cron jobs, queue workers, nightly
exports, you can't ping them,

150
00:09:48,781 --> 00:09:51,041
so you flip the direction.

151
00:09:51,148 --> 00:09:54,093
A heartbeat monitor
gives you a unique URL.

152
00:09:54,445 --> 00:09:57,183
The job calls it every time it finishes.

153
00:09:57,249 --> 00:10:01,347
If that call doesn't arrive
within the interval plus a

154
00:10:01,349 --> 00:10:03,400
grace period, you get alerted.

155
00:10:03,810 --> 00:10:08,194
The create command has the same shape
as before, just with a grace period.

156
00:10:08,790 --> 00:10:14,766
One detail, the CLI redacts the
heartbeat URL in its output by default

157
00:10:15,103 --> 00:10:17,218
because it's effectively a secret.

158
00:10:17,816 --> 00:10:24,043
Only use Dashdash reveal dash secrets
in the one pipeline step that writes it

159
00:10:24,058 --> 00:10:27,361
into the job secret store, nothing else.

160
00:10:28,388 --> 00:10:33,190
And I mentioned I would be honest about
whether a CLI is the wrong answer.

161
00:10:33,551 --> 00:10:37,375
A CLI in the pipeline is
great for per-service monitors

162
00:10:37,375 --> 00:10:39,065
created alongside a deploy.

163
00:10:39,712 --> 00:10:45,731
But if you want your whole fleet's
monitoring defined as desired state

164
00:10:45,997 --> 00:10:51,709
with drift detection and code review,
that's what declarative tools are for.

165
00:10:51,778 --> 00:10:56,950
If you have AI agents investigating
incidents, an MCP server is a better

166
00:10:56,965 --> 00:10:59,580
interface than having them shell out.

167
00:11:00,026 --> 00:11:03,721
And Uptime Robot has also an MCP.

168
00:11:04,250 --> 00:11:08,689
And for figuring out an incident
at 3:00 AM, a human in the

169
00:11:08,689 --> 00:11:10,609
dashboard beats any command.

170
00:11:11,105 --> 00:11:15,067
Whatever you choose, pick one
source of truth per monitor.

171
00:11:15,502 --> 00:11:19,712
The fastest way to get drift is a
pipeline that creates monitors which

172
00:11:19,728 --> 00:11:23,351
people then edit by hand in the UI.

173
00:11:24,527 --> 00:11:29,705
Finally, the monitoring you genuinely
can't template because it depends

174
00:11:29,734 --> 00:11:31,851
on things your service doesn't own.

175
00:11:32,547 --> 00:11:34,133
Third-party dependencies.

176
00:11:34,804 --> 00:11:36,818
Monitoring them is the easy part.

177
00:11:36,851 --> 00:11:42,417
Uptime Robot already tracks
the status of more than 6.3K+

178
00:11:42,736 --> 00:11:44,207
third-party services.

179
00:11:44,586 --> 00:11:49,874
What a template can't know is which
of those your service depends on.

180
00:11:50,274 --> 00:11:54,857
Nothing in the repo says,
checkout needs Stripe and SendGrid.

181
00:11:55,263 --> 00:11:58,067
Someone who knows the
service has to pick them.

182
00:11:58,765 --> 00:12:02,098
Secondly, user journeys
that cross services.

183
00:12:02,628 --> 00:12:05,926
Checkout only works if four services work.

184
00:12:06,819 --> 00:12:07,880
Alert routing.

185
00:12:08,571 --> 00:12:12,113
Who gets woken up depends
on people, rotations, and

186
00:12:12,114 --> 00:12:14,981
escalation policies, not on code.

187
00:12:15,542 --> 00:12:16,638
And thresholds.

188
00:12:17,244 --> 00:12:22,345
A sensible response to a response time
threshold needs real traffic, and a

189
00:12:22,346 --> 00:12:25,035
brand-new service doesn't have any yet.

190
00:12:25,637 --> 00:12:28,927
The template gets you a
solid baseline on day one.

191
00:12:29,309 --> 00:12:33,372
This need a human, and that's
fine, just make them a conscious

192
00:12:33,372 --> 00:12:35,671
decision, not a forgotten one.

193
00:12:36,013 --> 00:12:40,755
And, to sum up, putting monitoring
on the golden path as a deploy

194
00:12:40,763 --> 00:12:45,964
step, template the policy, and let
each service supply its identity.

195
00:12:46,323 --> 00:12:51,531
Make the step safe to rerun with the
right key and explicit flags, and keep

196
00:12:51,544 --> 00:12:53,945
humans for the things you don't own.

197
00:12:54,731 --> 00:12:58,628
Do that, and new services
arrive already being watched.

198
00:12:59,024 --> 00:12:59,767
Thank you.

199
00:12:59,816 --> 00:13:01,855
thank you so much for attending my talk.

200
00:13:01,917 --> 00:13:08,293
The CLI is open source on GitHub at
Uptime Robot slash Uptime Robot CLI.

201
00:13:08,663 --> 00:13:10,494
So take a look at the README.

202
00:13:10,531 --> 00:13:13,802
That's where all the examples
in this talk came from.

203
00:13:14,171 --> 00:13:19,131
I would love to hear how your team
handles this or how it doesn't, and

204
00:13:19,131 --> 00:13:21,036
enjoy the rest of the conferences.

205
00:13:21,087 --> 00:13:22,848
I'll be around for questions.

